.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt ECHO 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm echo
.Nd write arguments to the standard output
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n
.Op Ar string ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes each
.Ar string ,
separated by single spaces, and then a newline.
.Pp
The only option is
.Fl n ,
which leaves the newline off; it is recognised only as the first argument,
exactly, and once.
Anything else, however it begins, is written as it is.
A last
.Ar string
that ends in
.Ql \ec
also leaves the newline off, and those two characters with it.
No other backslash is special.
.Pp
Shells have an
.Ic echo
built-in, which may differ; for portable output with escapes, use
.Xr printf 1 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Write a prompt with no newline:
.Dl echo -n "Continue? "
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr printf 1 ,
.Xr sh 1
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2008 ,
which leaves both
.Fl n
and backslashes to the implementation.
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v2 .
